# Welcome to March 1, 2026

The Singularity is already embedded too deep to uninstall.

Hours after Trump [ordered every federal agency to cease using Anthropic](https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trump-says-he-is-directing-federal-agencies-cease-use-anthropic-technology-2026-02-27/) and the Pentagon [designated the company a supply chain risk](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/ip-law/trump-orders-us-government-to-drop-anthropic-after-pentagon-feud), U.S. Central Command [used Anthropic's Claude for intelligence assessments, target identification, and battle simulations](https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-used-anthropics-claude-ai-in-iran-strikes-hours-after-trumps-ban-report-iran-israel-us-war-donald-trump-vs-anthropic-11153230) during Operation Epic Fury — the joint U.S.-Israeli strike that [killed Iranian Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei](https://www.npr.org/2026/02/28/g-s1-112026/why-is-the-u-s-attacking-iran) and most of Iran's senior military leadership. The AI that was [too dangerous for government use on Friday](https://www.wired.com/story/anthropic-supply-chain-risk-shockwaves-silicon-valley/) was targeting Iranian command infrastructure by Saturday morning. Rep. Seth Moulton [put it plainly](https://x.com/RepMoulton/status/2028128404365848785): "Either they used tech that is a natsec risk during military action, or they lied in the first place." The Pentagon has a six-month phase-out window because Claude is [too deeply integrated into military workflows](https://ibtimes.co.uk/us-military-ai-middle-east-strikes-trump-ban-1782259) to remove overnight — the same military that just classified the company as a threat to national security.

**The replacement deal came with the same red lines.** OpenAI [published its Pentagon contract](https://openai.com/index/our-agreement-with-the-department-of-war/) on Saturday, claiming it contains [more guardrails than any previous classified AI deployment](https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/openai-details-layered-protections-us-defense-department-pact-2026-02-28/) — including Anthropic's — with three explicit prohibitions: no mass domestic surveillance, no autonomous weapons, no social credit systems. Sam Altman ran [a Saturday night AMA on X](https://x.com/sama/status/2027957684625150444) calling the Anthropic blacklisting "an extremely scary precedent," while also noting OpenAI [moved quickly to de-escalate](https://businessinsider.com/takeaways-sam-altman-openai-pentagon-deal-ask-me-anything-2026-3). On the question of whether the Pentagon gave OpenAI the terms it refused Anthropic, Altman was blunt: ["I don't know the details of what they received."](https://x.com/sama/status/2027960563285037540) The company that said yes with caveats got the classified networks; the company that said the same thing with conviction got the Huawei treatment. OpenAI still [isn't approved for classified work](https://www.implicator.ai/openais-pentagon-deal-claims-the-same-red-lines-that-got-anthropic-blacklisted/) and lacks infrastructure on military secure clouds — what Altman signed and when it takes effect, nobody outside the negotiations can say.

**The war is stress-testing more than AI contracts.** Israel dropped [over 1,200 munitions across 24 of Iran's 31 provinces](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/1/us-israel-attacks-on-iran-death-toll-and-injuries-live-tracker) in the first 24 hours. Iran [retaliated against 27 bases and Gulf cities](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/01/us-iran-live-updates-khamenei-death-trump-gulf-strikes.html) — Dubai's Palm Jumeirah, Jebel Ali port, and Burj Al Arab [all took damage](https://www.the-independent.com/news/world/middle-east/dubai-airport-attack-iran-us-gulf-bahrain-b2929651.html). The UAE reported [three dead and 58 injured](https://www.arabnews.com/node/2634845/middle-east) from 165 ballistic missiles and 541 drones. Three U.S. service members were killed. Brent crude [jumped 10% to $80 a barrel](https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/oil-jumps-10-iran-conflict-could-spike-100-barrel-analysts-say-2026-03-01/) in over-the-counter Sunday trading, with analysts predicting [$100 if the Strait of Hormuz closes](https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/01/business/oil-prices-us-attack-iran-vis). OPEC+ [agreed to raise output by 206,000 barrels per day](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/03/01/us-iran-live-updates-khamenei-death-trump-gulf-strikes.html) starting in April — a rounding error against an 8-to-10-million-barrel disruption scenario. The largest energy crisis in decades opened the same weekend the largest AI governance crisis reached its climax.

